WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.083
Roxy: Eggsy!

00:00:01.083 --> 00:00:02.013
Eggsy: Yours first, OK?

00:00:02.013 --> 00:00:02.847
Roxy: Yep.

00:00:05.655 --> 00:00:08.155
Being able to grip and hold on

00:00:08.155 --> 00:00:09.788
is virtually impossible.

00:00:09.788 --> 00:00:12.120
That parachute is designed for one person.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.562
Hi, I'm Mike Elliott, a
26-year combat veteran.

00:00:15.562 --> 00:00:17.170
I served 11 years with the US Army

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:18.670
Golden Knight Parachute Team.

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:21.108
I have over 16,000 skydives,

00:00:21.108 --> 00:00:23.760
6,000 tandems, 100 static-line jumps,

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:26.130
and about 20 military free-fall jumps.

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:28.293
Today we're going to be
looking at parachuting scenes

00:00:28.293 --> 00:00:31.210
in movies and judge how real they are.

00:00:34.679 --> 00:00:35.700
Oh, come on, come on, come on, come on.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.320
I think he did the correct procedures.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:40.200
With military free-fall
jumpers with combat equipment,

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.240
they have their O2 masks on, googles,

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.410
it's a lot of moving parts,

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:46.590
so there is a possibility that
something could come loose.

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:48.557
He takes about 15 seconds

00:00:48.557 --> 00:00:51.780
to fix his O2 pipe and
put it back to his helmet.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:54.690
You can hold your breath up
to a minute and still be OK,

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:56.790
so he had plenty of time to fix his mask

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:58.040
and still leave the aircraft.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.820
His bottom position is very correct here.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:04.920
He's in what we call a dive position.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.110
And it is so über-important

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:09.360
to have the correct body position.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.510
Basically, you have to learn
how to push air with your body

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:14.790
so the wind is basically
hitting him around his waist

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:17.040
and chest area, which allows that wind

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.260
to be symmetrical around his body

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:21.960
so he can gain stability
after he exits the aircraft.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.090
Automated voice: 25,000 feet.

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:27.420
They did a very great
job as a HALO jumper.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:31.320
Basically, HALO is "high
altitude, low opening,"

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:34.020
meaning you exit the
aircraft above 15,000 feet.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:37.100
You have to have some type
of breathing apparatus

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:39.291
in the aircraft as well as on your body.

00:01:39.291 --> 00:01:40.740
As far as the form is concerned,

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.570
there's no different form with HALO.

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:45.810
It's called the box-man
position or the arch position.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:48.450
Everything he's doing in
this scene is done correctly.

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:51.090
Your knees are bent, your
pelvis is pushed forward,

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:52.676
your arms are back.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:57.120
That is a pretty gnarly-looking storm.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.140
I have never had to jump
into clouds and lightning,

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:02.310
and it's not even approved

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:03.960
by the Federal Aviation Administration.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.000
With clouds, you cannot see the ground

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.310
and you can get struck by lightning.

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:09.785
And if you're flying with other jumpers,

00:02:09.785 --> 00:02:12.437
now you at a risk of
running into another person.

00:02:12.437 --> 00:02:15.750
And when you slam into another
person at 120 miles per hour,

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:17.266
it's going to be some serious injuries.

00:02:19.831 --> 00:02:23.370
Automated voice: 4,000.
Deploy. Deploy. Deploy.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:26.190
I do like that the body position

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:28.440
of both actors in this movie are correct.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:31.050
If you're unconscious, you're
going to be on your back,

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.420
because your arms and legs
are the lowest portions

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:36.480
of your body, so gravity
will put you on your back.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:39.810
I would rate this a high 7, for sure.

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:42.073
All those are very unique maneuvers

00:02:42.073 --> 00:02:43.290
that could possibly happen.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:51.330
[Mike laughing]

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:54.240
Well, very unrealistic scene.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.376
There is no way anyone

00:02:56.376 --> 00:02:58.650
could have made it through
that type of debris.

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:01.500
To bring it more home,
I was at an altitude

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:04.350
of about 14,000 feet, and
we had some icing conditions

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:05.938
when we stepped out of the airplane.

00:03:05.938 --> 00:03:07.800
And it was the most painful thing

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:09.210
that I think I've ever experienced.

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:12.000
It was like being shot in
the face with a BB gun.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.225
So if you can think about

00:03:13.225 --> 00:03:14.991
getting hit by a piece of that debris,

00:03:14.991 --> 00:03:17.610
there's no way I would've
left that building

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:19.276
with a parachute whatsoever.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:26.940
You could catch up with
someone in free fall.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.340
With that position she was
in, the full delta position,

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:30.540
you straighten your legs

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:32.970
and you pull your arms against your body,

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:34.980
so there's nothing out
there that's going to

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:37.170
create the wind from
flowing around your body.

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:41.520
You can get up to 250 to
280 miles an hour, easy.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.430
I'm not going to say this
particular episode was realistic.

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:47.880
For every action, there's
a reaction in free fall.

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:49.485
If you're looking up
trying to find someone,

00:03:49.485 --> 00:03:52.650
that is going to change
your whole body position.

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:54.240
I mean, it's going to change the airflow

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.806
of symmetry around your body,
so you're going to slow down

00:03:56.806 --> 00:03:58.281
and you're going to come out of that dive.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:03.390
I don't know of anyone that has

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:05.580
put a parachute on in midair.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:08.700
In order to put those leg
straps around your legs,

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:12.030
put your arms in that
harness at 120 miles an hour

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:14.220
with debris falling around you,

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:16.350
I don't believe that is possible.

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:17.843
I would say 1.

00:04:17.843 --> 00:04:20.904
Nothing in this clip, to
me, looks real whatsoever.

00:04:20.904 --> 00:04:23.943
[beeping]

00:04:28.713 --> 00:04:32.910
A total high five on this scene,
from the ramp to the exit.

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:35.670
With a military free-fall operation,

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:39.000
each group that's exiting an
aircraft has a team leader.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:40.230
And you're guiding all the guys,

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:42.540
so they are grouping around him

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:43.920
so they can see what's going on.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.890
And that's the same formation they stay in

00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:49.232
during their exit and as
they get out into free fall.

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:53.040
As you see here, they
jumping at nighttime.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:55.560
Everything gets really,
really difficult at nighttime

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:58.080
because you're either flying through NODs

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:00.907
or just with your naked eye,
and you can't see obstacles.

00:05:00.907 --> 00:05:02.340
You can't see the ground coming up.

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:05.430
And in the Army, you have
the round static-line jumps

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:07.260
from, like, the 82nd Airborne Division,

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:08.610
and you have the Army Parachute Team,

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:11.730
which are demonstrations
and competitive-type guys.

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:15.420
On the special-operations
side, the US Navy SEALs

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:18.330
or Delta Force or Special
Forces, these guys are trained

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:20.310
to put that parachute
where they need to put it

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:21.840
to do what they need to do best.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:25.540
So if these guys are illustrating
from a spec-ops side,

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:27.975
yes, that is something that
can definitely be done.

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:34.590
In this particular scene,
they're doing a HALO.

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:36.660
Special-operations soldiers,
their thing is to be

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:39.780
as stealthy and as quiet
and as dark as they can.

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:43.560
And the premise of a HALO
jump is soldiers can step out

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:47.070
up to 40 or 50 miles away at 25,000 feet,

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:49.528
open their parachute by 20,000 feet,

00:05:49.528 --> 00:05:53.550
and they can drift in quietly,
stealthily, undetected

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:54.930
from miles away.

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:57.300
For this, I don't know
why they would have smoke.

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:59.190
Especially how you can't
see smoke at nighttime.

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:02.580
It's mandatory to have
blinking lights on your body

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:04.020
when you're flying at nighttime,

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:06.630
so other aircraft can see you.

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:07.896
Special-operation guys,

00:06:07.896 --> 00:06:10.504
they're not going to have
any lights and flares

00:06:10.504 --> 00:06:13.350
because they're coming in as stealthily,

00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:14.460
as quiet as possible,

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:17.267
so they can do the mission
once they get on the ground.

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:23.730
The landing, as you see,
it's a extremely tight area.

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:26.416
The tight parachutes that are
military, special-op guys,

00:06:26.416 --> 00:06:29.107
it's called MC-4s, MC-5s.

00:06:29.107 --> 00:06:31.950
Very big, very docile parachutes.

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:35.693
They definitely can land on
buildings and tight areas.

00:06:35.693 --> 00:06:38.010
I don't think they would
land in something that tight.

00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:39.326
Not a bad-looking clip at all.

00:06:39.326 --> 00:06:41.332
Yeah, I would give them a 6.

00:06:41.332 --> 00:06:42.630
But I don't know how to parachute.

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:44.355
That's OK, we're going to tandem jump.

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:47.460
Trust me.

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:49.393
First of all, when was that moment

00:06:49.393 --> 00:06:51.600
that you put a tandem parachute

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:53.400
and a tandem harness
inside of the airplane?

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:54.960
Yeah, I was the team leader

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:57.751
for the tandem team on the
US Army Parachute Team.

00:06:57.751 --> 00:06:59.310
And I was selected to jump

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:01.080
with our former President Bush.

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:03.377
In order to do a tandem jump,

00:07:03.377 --> 00:07:07.830
it takes a good 15 to 20
minutes to don that harness

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:10.290
as well as his parachute system.

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:12.564
I mean, you have to watch a waiver video

00:07:12.564 --> 00:07:14.190
and go through the procedures,

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:16.980
because a licensed,
experienced tandem instructor

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:18.930
could take a person with no experience,

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:21.480
and he can go through the
thrills of free-falling.

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:24.660
They just didn't have
enough time to prepare

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:27.982
for a tandem operation while in flight.

00:07:27.982 --> 00:07:28.815
Ready?

00:07:28.815 --> 00:07:30.750
Do I sound ready?

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:31.659
Yep.

00:07:31.659 --> 00:07:32.940
[Emma screaming]

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:33.985
That is a correct exit,

00:07:33.985 --> 00:07:36.540
to use a seated position
to leap from the aircraft.

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:39.030
Normally if you're
doing a seated position,

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:42.150
you have someone that's
really nervous of the jump

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:43.650
or a little heavier,

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:45.420
so a seated position's the best way

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:48.173
to get them out of the door
into the relative wind.

00:07:48.173 --> 00:07:51.269
[Emma screaming]

00:07:51.269 --> 00:07:53.760
If you notice, there's a
cord coming out of the back

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:56.400
of Dwayne Johnson's parachute.
That is called a drogue.

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:58.708
Because of the speed of
two people together --

00:07:58.708 --> 00:08:00.508
normal speed of one person in free fall

00:08:00.508 --> 00:08:02.400
is about 120 miles an hour.

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:04.740
Well, for a tandem, that speed increases

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:06.930
to about 180 miles an hour.

00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:08.520
So you have to set that drogue out,

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:11.580
which is basically a
smaller round parachute

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:14.113
to slow you back down
to 120 miles an hour.

00:08:21.082 --> 00:08:22.890
You see him here tapping her.

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:26.070
That tap is for her to bring
her arms back into her body,

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:27.960
because he's getting ready
to open up the parachute.

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:30.175
You don't want the
passenger to grab handles

00:08:30.175 --> 00:08:31.650
during that opening process.

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:34.620
Because you have two
folks under one canopy,

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:36.362
the canopy is a lot bigger,

00:08:36.362 --> 00:08:39.024
so things have to open a lot higher.

00:08:39.024 --> 00:08:42.711
A normal tandem opening
procedure starts at 5,500 feet.

00:08:42.711 --> 00:08:43.640
If you're doing a solo jump,

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:46.119
you can take it down as low as 2,500 feet.

00:08:46.119 --> 00:08:47.510
And if you noticed in the clip,

00:08:47.510 --> 00:08:50.735
the drogue kind of collapses
and the parachute comes out,

00:08:50.735 --> 00:08:52.800
very proper opening technique.

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:56.383
I'm going to rate this about a 4,

00:08:56.383 --> 00:08:58.366
simply because the training technique

00:08:58.366 --> 00:08:59.524
inside of the aircraft.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:07.181
Wow. I mean, every time I
see a static-line operation

00:09:07.181 --> 00:09:09.300
at this depth, it gives me chill bumps

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:11.850
to think about soldiers in World War II.

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:13.440
The forefathers of parachuting.

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:16.350
With static-line jumping,
it's the most expedient way

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:17.880
to put soldiers on the battlefield.

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:20.013
If you look at some of the actual footage

00:09:20.013 --> 00:09:21.180
that happened on D-Day

00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:23.610
and Market Garden, that's
basically what you see.

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:27.610
Numerous, numerous parachutes
in the air at any given time.

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:33.690
So, this is absolutely 100% correct.

00:09:33.690 --> 00:09:36.623
I was actually an airborne
instructor for three years,

00:09:36.623 --> 00:09:39.482
where I trained parachutists
how to become paratroopers.

00:09:39.482 --> 00:09:42.358
And the best way to get out
the aircraft is basically

00:09:42.358 --> 00:09:44.850
you pushing the guy in
front of you out the door

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:46.740
so you can get out the
door as quick as possible.

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:49.590
And as you exit, you get into
a good, tight body position,

00:09:49.590 --> 00:09:51.332
which is your hands over your harness,

00:09:51.332 --> 00:09:52.373
feet and knees together.

00:09:52.373 --> 00:09:55.350
And that static line actually
pulls your parachute out,

00:09:55.350 --> 00:09:57.660
and your parachute opens automatically.

00:09:57.660 --> 00:09:59.280
I'm going to rate this as a 10.

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:02.010
Just amazing any and every time I see

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:04.627
what the guys done for
us back in World War II.

00:10:08.310 --> 00:10:10.560
There are so many different facets

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:12.540
of maneuvering your body through the air.

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:13.740
It's called free-flying.

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:16.680
You can fly on your feet,
you can fly on your head,

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:17.820
you can fly in a ball,

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:20.400
and it's all about learning
how to maneuver air

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:22.680
with your body in whatever
position you're in.

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:25.272
So yes, this is very, very
accurate, what he's doing.

00:10:27.445 --> 00:10:28.495
F---! Whoa!

00:10:28.495 --> 00:10:29.736
If we can stop here for a second.

00:10:29.736 --> 00:10:33.600
They are portraying that
you can talk in the air.

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:37.080
At 120 miles an hour,
falling 176 feet per second,

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:40.855
you cannot hear anything but
the air rushing past your ears.

00:10:45.285 --> 00:10:48.540
[Johnny laughing]

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:50.280
Wow, what a long free fall.

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:52.521
So, I don't know what altitude

00:10:52.521 --> 00:10:54.246
they exited the aircraft from,

00:10:54.246 --> 00:10:56.460
but obviously it was
not above 15,000 feet,

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:58.500
because they don't have O2 masks on.

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:00.075
At 12,000 feet, you're looking at

00:11:00.075 --> 00:11:02.520
about 45 seconds of free-fall time.

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:04.937
So, here we are, still falling.

00:11:04.937 --> 00:11:07.512
So, wow, they must've got
up really, really high.

00:11:12.410 --> 00:11:15.300
You're a speed star!

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:17.400
A lot of this happens
on the Golden Knights.

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:20.470
The Golden Knights have a
free-fall-formation team,

00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:21.780
a eight-way team that go out

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:23.760
and do these geometrical formations.

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:27.330
This formation we're looking
at is called a star formation,

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:28.890
and what they're doing is very correct.

00:11:28.890 --> 00:11:30.960
As you see, he's kind of
coming in nice and slow.

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:33.450
You have to slow your
body down to approach that

00:11:33.450 --> 00:11:35.550
to make sure you don't
take that formation out.

00:11:35.550 --> 00:11:38.100
When you see the guys that are
grabbing each other's wrists,

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:39.510
that is called taking a dock

00:11:39.510 --> 00:11:41.693
on the person to your
left or to your right.

00:11:41.693 --> 00:11:43.710
In order to consider it
being a built formation,

00:11:43.710 --> 00:11:46.423
you have to hold on to another person,

00:11:46.423 --> 00:11:47.730
either by their jumpsuit

00:11:47.730 --> 00:11:49.500
or by their wrist or by their arms.

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:51.780
And then for them to
have inexperienced jumper

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:54.435
and make it to a formation
with no problems,

00:11:54.435 --> 00:11:55.680
that's never gonna happen.

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:58.140
The average person that can
build a formation this size

00:11:58.140 --> 00:11:59.700
probably has about 100 jumps.

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:01.830
When you're watching a movie that it shows

00:12:01.830 --> 00:12:03.750
a guy just putting on a parachute

00:12:03.750 --> 00:12:05.070
and jumping out of an airplane,

00:12:05.070 --> 00:12:08.580
that is nowhere near close
to absolutely happening.

00:12:08.580 --> 00:12:10.673
That's a lot of time, a lot of training

00:12:10.673 --> 00:12:12.930
that goes behind doing
a parachute operation.

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:15.080
So I'm going to say a 5 for this one.

00:12:21.492 --> 00:12:24.420
Very realistic of what
kind of canopy he's flying.

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:28.592
He's flying a seven-cell
demonstration parachute

00:12:28.592 --> 00:12:30.390
made for pinpoint landing.

00:12:30.390 --> 00:12:32.040
And his exit procedure,

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:34.190
his hand when he deploys
the parachute's all correct.

00:12:34.190 --> 00:12:36.330
If I bring my hand over my head,

00:12:36.330 --> 00:12:38.580
that keeps the air
symmetrical around my body,

00:12:38.580 --> 00:12:40.180
so I still gain the stability

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:42.130
during the opening
process of my parachute.

00:12:46.038 --> 00:12:48.237
And that's how you enter a party.

00:12:48.237 --> 00:12:49.378
[laughing] I love it.

00:12:49.378 --> 00:12:51.150
I've jumped into The Pentagon.

00:12:51.150 --> 00:12:53.393
Done a lot of pretty unique places.

00:12:53.393 --> 00:12:56.953
You have to submit a air request

00:12:56.953 --> 00:12:58.650
through the Federal
Aviation Administration.

00:12:58.650 --> 00:13:00.810
They are going to be the
ones that approve you

00:13:00.810 --> 00:13:03.293
to jump and to land in
these different areas.

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:10.230
So, they are a little close
in this actual landing.

00:13:10.230 --> 00:13:14.190
By the FAA and United States
Parachute Association,

00:13:14.190 --> 00:13:17.400
you can land no closer
than 15 feet from people,

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:20.340
and you can fly no lower than
15 feet above their heads.

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:22.410
When you see him come
across the folks' heads

00:13:22.410 --> 00:13:23.851
and land right in front of them,

00:13:23.851 --> 00:13:25.320
you would have a little
more distance to that,

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:28.950
and you would have to have
a marked-off drop zone,

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:31.500
meaning with a safety
officer on the ground

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:33.933
controlling the crowd. Now, if you notice,

00:13:33.933 --> 00:13:36.480
he looks like he's just
pinching the controls

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:38.888
with his index finger and his thumb.

00:13:38.888 --> 00:13:39.960
I wouldn't advise that,

00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:42.090
because there's so much
pressure on those toggles.

00:13:42.090 --> 00:13:43.470
Toggles are very, very important.

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:45.210
It's like the brakes on a car.

00:13:45.210 --> 00:13:46.560
To keep those toggles secure,

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:48.183
you make a fist around your control.

00:13:48.183 --> 00:13:50.285
Or if you're like Golden Knights,

00:13:50.285 --> 00:13:53.550
we fly with our hands above
the toggles wrapped into a fist

00:13:53.550 --> 00:13:56.610
for a safe, soft, stand-up landing.

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:58.080
As you see him coming in here,

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:00.653
as soon as he pulled down
to stop his parachute,

00:14:00.653 --> 00:14:01.863
he would lose his toggles.

00:14:03.030 --> 00:14:04.650
What a wonderful day to become a knight.

00:14:04.650 --> 00:14:07.380
Yes, you can get out of
your equipment pretty quick.

00:14:07.380 --> 00:14:09.960
Basically it's only three
points of connection.

00:14:09.960 --> 00:14:12.180
You have the chest strap
and your two leg straps.

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:13.500
So if you have quick releases,

00:14:13.500 --> 00:14:15.240
as soon as your feet hit the ground,

00:14:15.240 --> 00:14:17.220
you pop your two leg connectors,

00:14:17.220 --> 00:14:18.592
you pop your chest strap,

00:14:18.592 --> 00:14:20.070
and you let the parachute
fall off your shoulders.

00:14:20.070 --> 00:14:22.260
Just a few minute things on his landing,

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:23.730
but everything else,

00:14:23.730 --> 00:14:27.270
as long as he had the correct
approvals, looks real good.

00:14:27.270 --> 00:14:28.689
So we're going to give him a 8.

00:14:28.689 --> 00:14:29.580
Roxy: Eggsy!

00:14:29.580 --> 00:14:30.925
Eggsy: Yours first, OK?

00:14:30.925 --> 00:14:32.160
Roxy: Yep, OK.

00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:38.430
Being able to grip and hold
on is virtually impossible.

00:14:38.430 --> 00:14:40.620
That parachute is designed for one person,

00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:41.970
and you're not hooked up properly

00:14:41.970 --> 00:14:43.200
as if you're doing a tandem.

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:44.790
But if I knew there was a possibility

00:14:44.790 --> 00:14:46.620
that parachute could save both our lives,

00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:49.503
then I would make that attempt to do that.

00:14:53.460 --> 00:14:55.518
I was trying to look and see

00:14:55.518 --> 00:14:57.660
what their altimeter reading was.

00:14:57.660 --> 00:14:58.650
You know, I couldn't really see.

00:14:58.650 --> 00:15:00.720
I see it's flashing "LOW ALTITUDE,"

00:15:00.720 --> 00:15:03.300
but we have no idea of how low they are

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:05.040
before they open up their main parachute.

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:08.160
2,500 feet is when you open
up your main parachute.

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:11.550
It takes time for those
things to fully open.

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:13.995
With today's parachute systems,

00:15:13.995 --> 00:15:16.650
we have what we call a
automatic opening device.

00:15:16.650 --> 00:15:19.050
It's going to open up the
reserve parachute for you,

00:15:19.050 --> 00:15:21.930
and that is designed to
open at a low altitude.

00:15:21.930 --> 00:15:25.110
So your main parachute, which
they are using in this scene,

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:28.342
they could open up at
that low of an altitude,

00:15:28.342 --> 00:15:29.658
is not very realistic.

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:37.438
To have someone attached
to you and land on a target

00:15:37.438 --> 00:15:38.760
that's not doing a tandem,

00:15:38.760 --> 00:15:39.750
absolutely impossible.

00:15:39.750 --> 00:15:41.520
This is called parachute accuracy.

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:42.930
Two people under one parachute,

00:15:42.930 --> 00:15:45.000
somebody's going to break
some ankles for sure.

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:46.320
In that case they should've did

00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:48.690
what we call a parachute landing fall,

00:15:48.690 --> 00:15:51.120
where you hit the balls of
your feet, calves, thighs,

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:53.430
and your buttocks, which is
the softest part of your body.

00:15:53.430 --> 00:15:54.872
So trying to land

00:15:54.872 --> 00:15:57.005
and roll to those soft sides of your body

00:15:57.005 --> 00:15:59.310
is kind of what they did in this scene.

00:15:59.310 --> 00:16:00.570
But there's just no way

00:16:00.570 --> 00:16:02.390
that you can even see around that person

00:16:02.390 --> 00:16:05.370
enough to fly that
parachute to a target area.

00:16:05.370 --> 00:16:07.611
I'm going to rate these guys at a 5.

00:16:10.980 --> 00:16:13.890
I love the fact that they did highlight

00:16:13.890 --> 00:16:17.310
on his parachute not
being hooked up properly.

00:16:17.310 --> 00:16:19.560
What we have here is
three-ring release system

00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:20.550
on his chest strap.

00:16:20.550 --> 00:16:24.420
You see the two conduits hanging
out of his main lift web,

00:16:24.420 --> 00:16:27.388
which kind of started
the whole malfunction

00:16:27.388 --> 00:16:29.096
that he was sabotaged.

00:16:33.363 --> 00:16:34.725
Cut away!

00:16:36.706 --> 00:16:38.085
So, he wasted a lot of time

00:16:38.085 --> 00:16:41.130
trying to fight this malfunction.

00:16:41.130 --> 00:16:42.993
If at any given time

00:16:42.993 --> 00:16:45.090
look up and this thing
is not flying properly,

00:16:45.090 --> 00:16:46.770
you have to start your cutaway procedures.

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:47.993
Meaning you have two handles:

00:16:47.993 --> 00:16:50.970
you have a cutaway handle to
get rid of the bad parachute,

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:52.578
and then you have your secondary handle,

00:16:52.578 --> 00:16:54.936
which is going to deploy your auxiliary,

00:16:54.936 --> 00:16:56.086
your reserve parachute.

00:16:56.086 --> 00:16:59.340
So he's sitting here, he's
playing with this canopy,

00:16:59.340 --> 00:17:01.740
and he's losing altitude and time quickly.

00:17:01.740 --> 00:17:03.953
Anything lower than 800 or 900 feet,

00:17:03.953 --> 00:17:05.700
you stand a chance of it not opening.

00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:07.882
So now, by the time he does cut it away,

00:17:07.882 --> 00:17:10.748
he's at a lower altitude
to get his reserve out.

00:17:13.410 --> 00:17:14.243
And as you can see,

00:17:14.243 --> 00:17:16.590
then now his reserve
handle was not working.

00:17:16.590 --> 00:17:19.483
When you have a cutaway and
you have to fire your reserve,

00:17:19.483 --> 00:17:21.570
there's really not a lot of tension.

00:17:21.570 --> 00:17:24.510
So you have the strength to
pull that reserve handle out.

00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:26.112
Before I pick up my parachute,

00:17:26.112 --> 00:17:30.008
I do a overall inspection
of it each and every time.

00:17:30.008 --> 00:17:32.250
That's just safety
procedures in the sport.

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:34.662
So, very unlikely that
you're going to have

00:17:34.662 --> 00:17:36.829
a sabotaged parachute nowadays.

00:17:41.010 --> 00:17:43.020
Yeah, the guy would've never
made it to his rescuer.

00:17:43.020 --> 00:17:45.600
For about every four seconds in free fall,

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:47.250
you lose about 1,000 feet.

00:17:47.250 --> 00:17:50.340
So as we watch him go
through his malfunction,

00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:52.312
he's probably in free fall
for three or four seconds

00:17:52.312 --> 00:17:54.540
trying to get that reserve to open up.

00:17:54.540 --> 00:17:58.410
And a guy is going to cut away
from his main open parachute

00:17:58.410 --> 00:18:00.840
to come down and try to save him.

00:18:00.840 --> 00:18:02.280
There's not enough altitude in the world

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:04.560
for him to do that in
this given situation.

00:18:04.560 --> 00:18:07.110
By that time, he would've
impacted the ground, for sure.

00:18:07.110 --> 00:18:08.460
I'm going to rate this at a 7.

00:18:08.460 --> 00:18:10.590
We know at the bottom end
it got a little dramatic.

00:18:10.590 --> 00:18:14.850
My favorite parachuting scene
is in "The Longest Day."

00:18:14.850 --> 00:18:18.060
If you ever go to France, you
can see that particular church

00:18:18.060 --> 00:18:19.830
that still has a parachute hung up.

00:18:19.830 --> 00:18:23.490
I truly love all of
the World War II movies

00:18:23.490 --> 00:18:26.730
that show what our
soldiers were up against

00:18:26.730 --> 00:18:27.750
during that time frame.

00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:28.620
Thanks for watching!

00:18:28.620 --> 00:18:32.761
If you enjoyed this video, why
not click on the video above?

